Fluids for which the rate of deformation is linearly proportional to the shear stress are called Newtonian fluids after Sir Isaac Newton, who expressed it first in 1687. Most common fluids such as water, air, gasoline, and oils are Newtonian fluids. Blood and liquid plastics are examples of non-Newtonian fluids. In one-dimensional shear flow of Newtonian fluids, shear stress can be expressed by the linear relationship
